'' 'asc( w, n )' OVER A WIDE OPERAND has its own entry point -- measured,
'' 'fb_WstrAsc( (uint16*)W$0, 1ll )', with the position following the POINTER width as
'' the narrow fb_ASC's does. Asked for a descriptor, a wstring had none and every one
'' of them declined.
''
'' AND A WIDE STRING CONSTANT HAD NO TEXT AT ALL. 'const S = wstr( "ab" )' has a type --
'' 'WSTRING * 3' -- but no storage and no address, and the constant folder only read a
'' literal or a concatenation, never a conversion. So 'w = S' declined too, with
'' nothing to say why. The conversion changes the width, not the characters, so the
'' constant's text is its argument's and a wide literal is what the width is for.
''
'' string/asc.bas and string/asc-utf16le.bas: 21 declines each down to 4.
'' boolean/boolean_wstr.bas: 24 down to 0.
'' NOT FOLDED, and it shows: fbc constant-folds 'asc' over a CONSTANT to its value and
'' tiko-lang calls the runtime for it, so fbc emits one fewer call of each. Same answer,
'' more code -- the same divergence chr and str already have.
'' TEST: cli
'' ARGS: --dump-ir tests/mir/src/wide-const-asc.bas
'' EXPECT-EXIT: 0
'' EXPECT-OUT: verify: ok
'' EXPECT-OUT: 0 statements not lowered
'' EXPECT-OUT: fb_WstrAsc
'' GOLDEN: expected/wide-const-asc.out
